.Sh DESCRIPTION
Signed Certificate Timestamps (SCTs) are defined by RFC 6962, Section
3.2. They constitute a promise by a Certificate Transparency (CT) log to
publicly record a certificate.
By cryptographically verifying that a log did indeed issue an SCT, some
confidence can be gained that the certificate is publicly known.
.Pp
An internal representation of an SCT can be created in one of two ways.
The first option is to create a blank SCT, using
.Fn SCT_new ,
and then populate it using:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Fn SCT_set_version
to set the SCT version.
.Pp
Only SCT_VERSION_V1 is currently supported.
.It
.Fn SCT_set_log_entry_type
to set the type of certificate the SCT was issued for:
.Pp
.Sy CT_LOG_ENTRY_TYPE_X509
for a normal certificate.
.Sy CT_LOG_ENTRY_TYPE_PRECERT
for a pre-certificate.
.It
.Fn SCT_set0_log_id
or
.Fn SCT_set1_log_id
to set the LogID of the CT log that the SCT came from.
.Pp
The former takes ownership, whereas the latter makes a copy.
See RFC 6962, Section 3.2 for the definition of LogID.
.It
.Fn SCT_set_timestamp
to set the time the SCT was issued (time in milliseconds since the Unix
Epoch).
.It
.Fn SCT_set_signature_nid
to set the NID of the signature.
.It
.Fn SCT_set0_signature
or
.Fn SCT_set1_signature
to set the raw signature value.
.Pp
The former takes ownership, whereas the latter makes a copy.
.It
.Fn SCT_set0_extensions
or
.Sy SCT_set1_extensions
to provide SCT extensions.
.Pp
The former takes ownership, whereas the latter makes a copy.
.El
.Pp
Alternatively, the SCT can be pre-populated from the following data
using
.Fn SCT_new_from_base64 :
.Bl -bullet
.It
The SCT version (only SCT_VERSION_V1 is currently supported).
.It
The LogID (see RFC 6962, Section 3.2), base64 encoded.
.It
The type of certificate the SCT was issued for:
.Sy CT_LOG_ENTRY_TYPE_X509
for a normal certificate.
.Sy CT_LOG_ENTRY_TYPE_PRECERT
for a pre-certificate.
.It
The time that the SCT was issued (time in milliseconds since the Unix
Epoch).
.It
The SCT extensions, base64 encoded.
.It
The SCT signature, base64 encoded.
.El
.Pp
.Fn SCT_set_source
can be used to record where the SCT was found (TLS extension, X.509
certificate extension or OCSP response).
This is not required for verifying the SCT.
.Pp
.Fn SCT_free
frees the specified SCT. If the argument is NULL, nothing is done.
.Pp
.Fn SCT_LIST_free
frees the specified stack of SCTs.
If the argument is NULL, nothing is done.
.Sh NOTES
Some of the setters return int, instead of void.
These will all return 1 on success, 0 on failure.
They will not make changes on failure.
.Pp
All of the setters will reset the validation status of the SCT to
SCT_VALIDATION_STATUS_NOT_SET (see
.Xr SCT_validate 3 Ns ).
.Pp
.Fn SCT_set_source
will call
.Fn SCT_set_log_entry_type
if the type of certificate the SCT was issued for can be inferred from
where the SCT was found.
For example, an SCT found in an X.509 extension must have been issued
for a pre- certificate.
.Pp
.Fn SCT_set_source
will not refuse unknown values.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SCT_set_version
returns 1 if the specified version is supported, 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn SCT_set_log_entry_type
returns 1 if the specified log entry type is supported, 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn SCT_set0_log_id
and
.Sy SCT_set1_log_id
return 1 if the specified LogID is a valid SHA-256 hash, 0 otherwise.
Additionally,
.Sy SCT_set1_log_id
returns 0 if malloc fails.
.Pp
.Sy SCT_set_signature_nid
returns 1 if the specified NID is supported, 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Sy SCT_set1_extensions
and
.Sy SCT_set1_signature
return 1 if the supplied buffer is copied successfully, 0 otherwise
(i.e.
if malloc fails).
.Pp
.Sy SCT_set_source
returns 1 on success, 0 otherwise.
